  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1253 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThere is a threat of heavy rainfall and localized flash flooding \nmainly Thursday morning through Friday morning across the western \nand northern parts of our area. The overall pattern will be a strong \nslow moving upper level storm system moving out of the southwest US \nwith a meridional south-southwest flow over the Southern High \nPlains and favorable jet stream dynamics(RRQ at 250 MB). At the \nsurface, our area will remain in the warm sector ahead of a cold \nfront through Thursday evening. For moisture, PW values will \nincrease to 1.75 to 2 inches, low level southerly flow will be \nadvecting copious amounts of low level moisture across the \nwestern part of the area, and some energy from remnants of \nHurricane Simon will be transported into this system too. In \naddition, about a 6 hour window of strong overrunning behind a \ncold front mainly across the southern Concho Valley and Crockett \nCounty for early Friday morning. \n\nFor rainfall amounts, there is a low to medium(30-70%) chance of \n1.5 inches or greater west of an Abilene to San Angelo to Ozona \nline with the highest chances and locally higher totals possible \nacross the northwest Big Country, northwest Concho Valley and \nCrockett County late Wednesday night through Friday morning, due \nto repeating rounds of thunderstorms. Also, the Day 3(ending 12Z \nFriday) Excessive Rainfall Outlook has a slight risk across much \nof area which means the main hazard will be localized flash \nflooding. Looks like for now, the more significant impacts will \njust remain west and north of our area across the Permain Basin \nand South Plains into Oklahoma, but will have to watch this \nclosely as mesoscale details will play a role on the where the \nheaviest rain bands set up. Please keep up with the latest weather\ninformation. The Pops will be medium to high chance during this \nevent. We begin to dry out this weekend, as this system weakens \nand shears out. Another storm system will bring a chance of rain \nfor the middle of next week.\n\nFor temperatures, it will be hot through Thursday and then a good \ncold front moves through Friday morning with breezy and cooler \nconditions. Highs will be in the 60s Friday and Saturday and lows in \nthe 40s this weekend. Expect a warming trend next week with highs a \nfew degrees above normal.",
